<section class="p-hero"><div class="p-hero-bg"><img src="/assets/img/photos/pittsburgh.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/pittsburgh-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/pittsburgh.webp 1200w" sizes="100vw" width="1200" height="892" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" data-parallax="0.15"></div><div class="wrap p-hero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><i aria-hidden="true">›</i><a href="/where-we-work/">Where we work</a><i aria-hidden="true">›</i><span aria-current="page">Pittsburgh</span></nav><p class="kicker">Southwestern PA</p><h1>Insurance Appraiser in Pittsburgh, PA</h1><p class="lede">Pittsburgh homes cling to slopes, stack up on retaining walls and sit in narrow hollows that fill fast in a downpour. That geography shapes almost every claim we review in Allegheny County.</p><div class="btn-row"><a class="btn btn-gold" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="i" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn btn-line-light" href="/contact-us/">Free claim review</a></div><dl class="hero-facts"><div><dt>County</dt><dd>Allegheny County</dd></div><div><dt>Housing</dt><dd>Hillside brick homes &amp; frame rows</dd></div><div><dt>Storm on record</dt><dd>Ivan remnants, Sept 2004</dd></div></dl></div></section>
<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose">
<div class="peril-box"><p class="peril-h">Common local claim risks</p><ul class="chips"><li>Landslides &amp; earth movement</li><li>Flash flooding</li><li>Hail &amp; thunderstorms</li><li>Frozen pipes</li><li>Fallen trees</li></ul></div>

<h2>A city built on hillsides</h2>
<p>Few American cities have as many steps, retaining walls and houses perched over a drop as Pittsburgh. Brick homes on the South Side Slopes, Polish Hill, Troy Hill and Spring Hill sit at angles that complicate every repair: scaffolding costs more, materials are carried by hand up public stairways, and a roof on a three-story rear elevation can't be reached the way a flat-lot estimate assumes. Brookline, Beechview and Dormont add streets of tight frame and brick homes, while Squirrel Hill, Shadyside and Highland Park have larger older houses with slate roofs, box gutters and stained-glass windows.</p>
<p>When the first estimate is built from generic software defaults, these access and material issues are often the first things to disappear. We price the work the way local contractors actually have to do it.</p>
<h2>Landslides, retaining walls and the earth movement exclusion</h2>
<p>Allegheny County's steep slopes and clay-heavy soils move, especially in very wet years like 2018, when landslides closed roads and threatened homes across the region. Most homeowner policies exclude earth movement, and many exclude or limit retaining walls. That doesn't always end the conversation. A claim may still involve covered damage, such as a water line break that saturated the slope, a fallen tree, or wind damage that happened at the same time, and the policy language decides how those pieces are treated.</p>
<p>Parts of the county also sit over old coal workings. Damage from underground mine collapse is typically excluded from homeowner coverage, but the state's Mine Subsidence Insurance program, run by the Pennsylvania Department of Environmental Protection, offers separate protection. Our guide to <a href="/policy-and-claim-guides/mine-subsidence-sinkholes-and-your-policy/">mine subsidence, sinkholes and your policy</a> explains the differences. For a coverage denial itself, talk to a Pennsylvania attorney; appraisal settles amounts, not coverage.</p>
<h2>Ivan and the creek-valley boroughs</h2>
<p>In September 2004, the remnants of Hurricane Ivan dumped heavy rain on western Pennsylvania. Girtys Run poured through Millvale, Chartiers Creek flooded Carnegie, and creek-side neighborhoods in Etna, Sharpsburg and the river towns took on water. Similar flash flooding has returned to Pittsburgh's hollows in later summer storms.</p>
<p>Flood from rising water is excluded under homeowner policies and goes through a separate flood policy, usually NFIP, with its own process; appraisal under your homeowner policy doesn't reach it. Covered pieces can include sewer backup (if endorsed), roof and window damage from wind, and water that entered from above. We help separate them so covered damage isn't lost in a blanket flood denial.</p>
<h2>Other Allegheny County claims we review</h2>
<ul>
<li>Hail and wind claims on slate, tile and older asphalt roofs, including matching on discontinued materials.</li>
<li>Frozen pipe bursts in older homes with plumbing on exterior walls. See our <a href="/property-appraisal-services/ice-dam-frozen-pipe-appraisal/">ice dam and frozen pipe appraisal</a> service.</li>
<li>Fire and smoke losses in attached rows in Lawrenceville and Bloomfield, where damage crosses into the next house.</li>
<li>Commercial and multifamily losses in the suburbs, from Mt. Lebanon and Bethel Park to Ross and McCandless.</li>
</ul>
<p>If your carrier's estimate doesn't add up, start with an <a href="/property-appraisal-services/insurance-estimate-audit/">insurance estimate audit</a>. If the gap is large and your policy includes an appraisal clause, we can represent you as your appraiser.</p>

<h2>Services available in Pittsburgh</h2>
<p>Every Pennsylvania Insurance Appraiser service is available to property owners in Pittsburgh and the rest of Allegheny County, including:</p>
<ul class="cols2"><li><a href="/property-appraisal-services/appraisal-demand-representation/">Appraisal Demand Representation</a></li><li><a href="/property-appraisal-services/home-insurance-claim-appraisal/">Homeowner Claim Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/property-appraisal-services/commercial-hoa-claim-appraisal/">Commercial &amp; HOA Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/property-appraisal-services/roof-hail-damage-appraisal/">Roof &amp; Hail Damage Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/property-appraisal-services/windstorm-tornado-appraisal/">Wind &amp; Tornado Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/property-appraisal-services/tropical-storm-remnant-appraisal/">Tropical Storm Remnant Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/property-appraisal-services/water-damage-claim-appraisal/">Water Damage Claim Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/property-appraisal-services/fire-smoke-damage-appraisal/">Fire &amp; Smoke Damage Appraisal</a></li></ul>
<h2>Questions from Pittsburgh property owners</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>My retaining wall collapsed after heavy rain. Is it covered?<span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Often not, because many policies exclude earth movement and limit retaining walls. But the cause matters, and related damage may be covered. Read the policy closely and talk to a Pennsylvania attorney about a coverage denial; appraisal only settles the amount of a covered loss.</p></div></details><details><summary>Do I need mine subsidence insurance in Allegheny County?<span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Parts of the county sit over old coal mines. Standard policies usually exclude mine subsidence, and the state&#x27;s MSI program offers separate coverage. Check whether your address is in an undermined area.</p></div></details><details><summary>Why is my hillside repair estimate so much higher than the insurer&#x27;s?<span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Access is often the difference: hand-carried materials, scaffolding on slopes and limited staging. Those are real costs, and a detailed estimate should show them.</p></div></details></div>
